>> Rog-o-matic showed the way: it was careful to step
>> back into its own footprints whenever possible.
> What's that?

When you're retreating, walk along the same wall as you did when you
first got to that room. That route has already been checked for traps.

>> Don't drink unIDed potions
> I always do this when I'm poison-resistant. Moreso if I have a
> unihorn.

At least dip-test them for polymorph.

>> don't read unIDed scrolls
> Confusion makes reading scrolls much safer, except for genocide.

But mush less useful, as well.

>> don't wear unPetStomped armor, or armor
>> that might be dangerous even when uncursed
> How can armor be dangerous when uncursed? I've never found armor with
> a negative enchantment that was uncursed. Are you implying
> encumberance?

Some headgear auto-curses when you put it on. That is, it's uncursed
before you put it on, but gets cursed when you do (dunce cap, for
example, IIRC).

>> don't read unblessed spellbooks
> Meh. I usually wait until XL 7.

Still, this is exactly what we call gambling. If it's a high-level
spellbook, you might not only lose it (while it could have served you
later), you may also get paralysed or end up in a tight spot somewhere
else on the level. Also, intelligence is just as important as XL when
trying to learn a spell.

> Usually takes 5-6 kicks. Succubi are just hit and miss and I'm
> not bothered by death by exhaustion at such an early level.

Again, gambling. It often works out wrong, which is what this discussion
is all about: avoiding *all* things that might kill you early. If you
don't mind about that, this discussion is moot.

>> don't drink from fountains
> At least until you are sure that you're ready to take on a water
> demon or water moccasins or you can quickly make it into a corridor.

The latter can never be guaranteed, since the entrance to that corridor
may be blocked by water.

> Now if Nethack implemented something where the other peacefuls gang
> up on the offending player, it would cut down on such activity.

I doubt it... It would just mean less alignment penalties.  :-)

>> don't sit on thrones
> Thrones aren't that dangerous.

But it *still* is a possible quick death, and this discussion was about
not doing things that might quickly kill you, thus surviving much longer
on the average.

> Don't forget! Never eat corpses of monsters you destroyed while blind.
> That dwarf or gnome corpse may be from a mummy not a living dwarf or
> gnome.

Depends on whether you saw it with telepathy, of course. But in general,
good advice.

>> don't pray if the maximum possible prayer timeout
>> hasn't expired, [...]
> Very wise

Except that you can never say for certain whether or not is has expired.
Even two thousand turns might still not be enough. Nevertheless, I'd
take my chances at 300 turns when fainting.  :-)

